## 2. Key Commands

### 2.1 Filesystem Navigation

```bash
pwd                         # Display the current directory
ls                          # List files and directories
ls -la                      # Show hidden files and detailed information
cd /etc                     # Move to /etc
cd ..                       # Move to the parent directory
cd ~                        # Move to the current user's home directory


2.2 File and Directory Operations
mkdir devops-lab            # Create a directory
mkdir -p project/logs       # Create nested directories
touch notes.md              # Create an empty file if needed
cat notes.md                # Display the file contents
less notes.md               # Read a file page by page
cp notes.md notes-backup.md # Copy a file
mv notes-backup.md backup.md # Rename a file
find . -name "*.md"         # Find Markdown files under the current directory

To remove files, first inspect the target:

ls -l notes.md              # Confirm the target file
rm notes.md                 # Delete the specified file

For directories, rmdir removes an empty directory. Recursive deletion with rm -r can remove many files, so inspect the path before using it.

2.3 Users, Groups, and Permissions
whoami                      # Display the current username
id                          # Display user ID and group memberships
groups                      # Display the current user's groups
ls -l                       # Inspect ownership and permissions
chmod 644 notes.md          # Owner can read/write; others can read
chmod 755 script.sh         # Owner has full permissions; others can read/execute
chmod u+x script.sh         # Add execute permission for the owner

Ownership changes generally require appropriate privileges:

sudo chown username:groupname file.txt  # Change owner and group; replace placeholders

Do not change ownership or permissions on system directories without understanding the impact.

2.4 Processes and Services
ps aux                      # List processes
ps aux | grep nginx         # Search the process list for nginx
top                         # Monitor running processes
pgrep -a nginx              # Find matching process IDs and command lines

On a system using systemd:

systemctl status nginx      # Check service status
sudo systemctl start nginx  # Start the service
sudo systemctl stop nginx   # Stop the service
sudo systemctl restart nginx # Restart the service
sudo systemctl enable nginx # Enable automatic startup at boot

Replace nginx with the actual service name. These examples assume that the service exists and that the current user has the required permissions.

For a process that must be stopped, identify its PID first:

kill PID                    # Request graceful termination; replace PID with a real process ID

Use forceful termination only as a last resort.

2.5 Environment Variables
echo "$HOME"                # Display the home directory
echo "$PATH"                # Display executable search paths
export APP_ENV=development  # Set an environment variable for this shell and child processes
printenv APP_ENV            # Display the variable if it is set
unset APP_ENV               # Remove the variable from this shell

Shell variables exported this way do not automatically persist across new terminal sessions.

2.6 Pipes, grep, and Logs
ps aux | grep nginx                     # Search process output
grep -n "ERROR" application.log         # Find ERROR lines with line numbers
grep -i "failed" application.log        # Search case-insensitively
tail -n 50 application.log              # Display the last 50 lines
tail -f application.log                 # Follow new log entries

On a system using systemd:

journalctl -u nginx                     # Read logs for a service
journalctl -u nginx -n 50               # Display the latest 50 entries
journalctl -u nginx -f                  # Follow new service log entries
2.7 Networking and HTTP
ip addr                                 # Display network interfaces and IP addresses
ip route                                # Display routing information
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8                       # Send four ICMP echo requests on Linux
getent hosts example.com                # Resolve a hostname on systems with getent
ss -tuln                                # List listening TCP/UDP sockets on Linux
curl -I https://example.com             # Inspect HTTP response headers
curl -v https://example.com             # Display detailed connection information

ip, ss, and getent are commonly available on Linux but may not be installed or available in all minimal environments. On macOS, alternatives include ifconfig, netstat, and dscacheutil -q host -a name example.com.

2.8 SSH Commands
ssh username@server-ip                  # Connect to a remote host
ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 username@server-ip # Select a private key
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "devops-training" # Generate an SSH key pair

Use the actual username and server address. Protect the private key with appropriate permissions and never upload it to GitHub.

2.9 Git Basics
git --version                            # Display the installed Git version
git clone https://github.com/OWNER/REPO.git # Clone a repository
cd REPO                                  # Enter the cloned repository
git status                               # Inspect the working tree
git diff                                 # Inspect unstaged changes
git add filename.md                     # Stage a selected file
git diff --cached                       # Inspect staged changes
git commit -m "Add Linux training notes" # Create a commit
git log --oneline                       # Display concise commit history

Replace OWNER, REPO, and filenames with actual values.

2.10 Branching and Merging
git branch                              # List local branches
git switch -c feature/linux-notes       # Create and switch to a branch
git status                              # Check branch and working tree state
git add filename.md                     # Stage the intended change
git commit -m "Document Linux commands" # Commit the change
git switch main                         # Switch to main
git merge feature/linux-notes           # Merge the feature branch into main
git branch -d feature/linux-notes       # Delete a merged local branch if no longer needed

Before switching branches or merging, ensure your work is committed or safely preserved.

2.11 Remote Repositories and Tags
git remote -v                           # Display configured remotes
git fetch origin                        # Download remote updates without merging
git branch -a                           # List local and remote-tracking branches
git push -u origin feature/linux-notes  # Publish a branch and set its upstream
git tag                                 # List tags
git tag v1.0.0                          # Create a lightweight tag
git show v1.0.0                         # Inspect the tag target

Push commands require suitable repository access. Always verify the intended branch and remote before pushing.

2.12 GitHub Pull Request Workflow
git switch -c docs/linux-notes          # Create a task-specific branch
git add day-01.md                       # Stage the notes file
git diff --cached                       # Review the staged changes
git commit -m "Add Day 1 Linux and Git notes" # Commit the changes
git push -u origin docs/linux-notes     # Publish the branch
